WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.197
Validity and accuracy are related but different.

00:00:03.197 --> 00:00:07.322
Validity asks whether a value fits a documented rule.

00:00:07.322 --> 00:00:11.077
Accuracy asks whether the value reflects reality.

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:18.387
A temperature can fall inside an allowed range and still be inaccurate because of a sensor problem or

00:00:18.387 --> 00:00:21.407
data-entry error. Validity is often easier to automate.

00:00:21.407 --> 00:00:28.057
Accuracy may require comparison with the source system, another measurement, or domain knowledge.

00:00:28.057 --> 00:00:34.272
This distinction prevents us from assuming that passing a validation rule proves the value is true.

00:00:34.272 --> 00:00:38.717
In practice, quality rules should be tied to a use case.

00:00:38.717 --> 00:00:45.442
A fifteen-minute delay may be acceptable for a monthly report but unacceptable for a real-time alert.

00:00:45.442 --> 00:00:53.227
Likewise, an unusual physiological value should often be flagged and investigated rather than automatically

00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:57.248
deleted. Good cleaning preserves evidence and documents uncertainty.
